13. FEATURE ELEVEN: GRAPHICS AND PERFORMANCE

Maintain a mobile-first performance budget.

Use the existing rendering architecture.

If the project uses Three.js, consider the following where appropriate:

- InstancedMesh for repeated environmental objects.
- Shared materials.
- Merged static geometry.
- Reduced shadow complexity.
- Distance-based detail.
- Limited particle counts.
- Efficient collision checks.
- Object pooling.
- Capped device pixel ratio.
- Proper GPU resource disposal.

Use InstancedMesh when many copies of the same geometry and material can be rendered efficiently.

Do not introduce complicated optimization systems without evidence that they are needed.

Provide three graphics presets:

LOW:

- Minimal shadows.
- Reduced environmental detail.
- Lower rendering resolution.

MEDIUM:

- Balanced shadows.
- Moderate environmental detail.
- Standard rendering resolution.

HIGH:

- More detailed environment.
- Improved shadows.
- Higher rendering resolution when the device can handle it.

Make the settings functional.

Changing the graphics preset should update the actual rendering configuration.

Do not allow the HIGH preset to make the game unusable on mobile hardware.

